What are the historical origins and significance of Rogation Days in the Christian church?
Rogation Days have their historical origins in the pagan Roman procession known as Robigalia, which was held to propitiate Robigus, the deity of agricultural disease, for protection of crops from wheat rust. The Christian major rogation replaced this pagan practice. The minor rogation days were introduced around AD 470 by Mamertus, bishop of Vienne, and were later adopted into the Roman rite by Pope Leo III. The significance of Rogation Days lies in their observance of days of prayer and fasting, processions, and the Litany of the Saints, with the purpose of beseeching God for protection from calamities and appeasement of his anger.
