What is the historical context of the farthing coin, including its design and production process?
The historical context of the farthing coin includes its design and production process as follows:

The farthing coin was a coin of the Kingdom of England worth one quarter of a penny, of a pound sterling. It was first minted in England in the 13th century, initially in silver, but later in copper. The design of the farthing coin typically featured a cross on the reverse, and it was similar in design to the penny but smaller in size.

Initially, the farthing coins were minted in the short-cross style during the reign of King Henry III (1216-1272). However, it was previously thought that the first silver farthings were produced in the reign of King Edward I (1272-1307). Recent discoveries have revealed that five examples of silver farthings were produced during the reign of King Henry III, which were considered to be trials due to the lack of known examples and documentary evidence.

The farthing coin continued to be produced until 1956, when it ceased to be struck. It was demonetized from 1 January 1961.
