What is the typical structure of the calyx in a flower, and how does it relate to the other parts of the flower?
The typical structure of the calyx in a flower of the genus Roscoea is a tube-shaped outer calyx, which is described as having a specific shape and number of lobes, varying slightly across different species. 

In general, the calyx is described as having a two- or three-toothed apex (Roscoea forrestii) or a bluntly two-lobed apex (Roscoea nepalensis). The calyx is also mentioned as being longer than the bracts that subtend the flowers (Roscoea tumjensis), and in some cases, the length of the calyx is equal to or slightly shorter than the bracts (Roscoea nepalensis and Roscoea forrestii).
