What is the historical context and significance of the poem 'Auld Lang Syne'?
The poem "Auld Lang Syne" was written by Robert Burns in 1788 and is a Scots-language poem set to the tune of a traditional folk song. It is well known in many countries, especially in the English-speaking world, and is traditionally sung to bid farewell to the old year at the stroke of midnight on New Year's Eve. The poem's historical context and significance lie in its use as a way to reflect on past times and relationships, and to bid farewell to the past year, while also looking forward to the new one. It has become a cultural phenomenon, with its melody being used for various occasions, including funerals, graduations, and other endings/new beginnings.
