What is the culinary origin and historical context of the Italian dessert 'Zuppa Inglese'?
The Italian dessert 'Zuppa Inglese' has a complex and uncertain culinary origin and historical context. According to one theory, it originated in the sixteenth century kitchens of the Dukes of Este in Ferrara, where they attempted to recreate the English trifle they had enjoyed in England. Another theory suggests that it was invented by Neapolitan pastrycooks in the 19th century as a tribute to Lord Nelson after his victory over Napoleon in 1798. The name 'Zuppa Inglese' translates to 'English soup' in Italian, which may refer to its similarity to English trifle or be a dialectical corruption of the verb 'inzuppare', meaning 'to dunk', as the sponge cake or Ladyfingers are dipped in liqueur. Recipes for this dessert first appeared in the regions of Emilia-Romagna, Marches, Umbria, and Latium in the late 19th century.
